  "prompt_text": "This figure is a world map summarizing how climate-related factors are expected to affect roads in different regions. Small stacked symbol boxes are placed across continents and islands, indicating which pavement problems are relevant at each location. The main message is that road damage is linked to three broad drivers—higher temperature, increased precipitation, and sea-level rise—and that the dominant impacts vary geographically.\n\nAcross many inland and northern locations, red symbols are common, showing heat-related pavement deterioration such as more rutting and buckling. Purple symbols appear widely in tropical, coastal, and wetter regions, indicating precipitation-driven rutting, cracking, and washouts. Blue symbols cluster along coasts and island areas, reflecting damage associated with sea-level rise, including cracking and washouts. Some places show several stacked symbols at once, suggesting multiple climate stressors acting together rather than a single hazard.\n\nOverall, the map conveys a global pattern of widespread but region-specific road vulnerability: hotter areas seem more exposed to thermal deformation, wetter regions to rainfall-related failure, and low-lying coastal zones to sea-level-rise impacts. The visual emphasizes that transportation infrastructure faces overlapping climate risks worldwide.",
